      "id": 960,
      "options": {
        "content": "<div style=\"padding:8px 0;\">\n  <div style=\"font-size:14px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px;\">How to Use This Dashboard</div>\n  <div style=\"font-size:13px;color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.6;\">\n    • <b>Forecast Valid Time:</b> Use the <b>Forecast Valid Time</b> dropdown to select\n    the hour you want to view. The map re-fetches automatically.\n    <br/>\n    • <b>Interaction:</b> Pan by dragging, zoom with scroll wheel or pinch. Hover over\n    any grid cell to see its exact probability value in the tooltip.\n    <br/>\n    • <b>Rendering:</b> Each 1°×1° grid cell is rendered as a filled rectangle\n    (identical to matplotlib's <code>pcolormesh</code>). No interpolation or smoothing is\n    applied — you see the native model resolution. Cell borders are hidden.\n    <br/>\n    • <b>Color Scale:</b> Blue = low probability → Yellow = moderate → Red = high.\n    12 threshold steps matching the dashboard's standard BlYlRd palette.\n    <br/>\n    • <b>Data Flow:</b> Browser → /geojson/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;model&gt;/&lt;time&gt; → tile-server\n    container → PostgreSQL <code>forecast_grid_data</code>. GeoJSON is cached for\n    5 minutes server-side. Response size: ~275 KB uncompressed, ~60 KB gzipped.\n    <br/>\n    • <b>Native Resolution:</b> 1°×1° (~111 km), 1,836 cells per hour,\n    covering Europe 35°–70° N, 10° W–40° E. Cells auto-resize if a finer-\n    resolution grid is ingested.\n  </div>\n</div>",
        "mode": "html"
      },
